```ts
const rows = [
  { name: 'Ada', active: true, qty: 2 },
  { name: 'Lin', active: false, qty: 5 },
]

// Same transform, three shapes
const a = rows.filter((r) => r.active).map((r) => r.name)
const b = rows.reduce<string[]>((acc, r) => {
  if (r.active) acc.push(r.name)
  return acc
}, [])
const c: string[] = []
rows.forEach((r) => {
  if (r.active) c.push(r.name) // side-effect rebuild of map+filter
})
// a and b (if we copy) are ['Ada']; c mutates c in place

// ❌ mutation
rows.sort((x, y) => x.name.localeCompare(y.name))
```

`filter`+`map` states intent. `reduce` is one pass when you already know you need it. `forEach`+`push` hides that you wanted `map`. `sort` mutates `rows`.

